<div>
    <div class="mb-16">
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white sm:text-5xl mb-6">
            <span class="bg-gradient-to-r from-red-400 via-amber-400 to-orange-400 bg-clip-text text-transparent">OAuth Client</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-8">
            Sign in with Apple, GitHub, Google, GitLab, Discord, LinkedIn or Microsoft. Scaffolded by
            <code class="text-cyan-400">soli generate oauth</code>
            &mdash; the opposite of
            <a href="/docs/security/oidc-provider" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">OIDC provider</a>
            (your app as the IdP).
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Prerequisites</h2>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-12">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-bash text-sm">soli generate auth          # User + sessions (required)
soli generate oauth github  # and/or apple, google, gitlab, discord, linkedin, microsoft
soli db:migrate up</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">What gets generated</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-12">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Path</th>
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Role</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-400">
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">app/models/oauth_identity.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">Links User &harr; provider + uid</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">app/services/oauth_sso.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>OauthSso</code>: state CSRF, PKCE, re-authentication, and which account an identity signs in</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">app/services/github_oauth.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">GitHub authorize / token / profile + verified email, PKCE</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">app/services/google_oauth.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">Google OIDC code + PKCE</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">app/services/{apple,gitlab,discord,linkedin,microsoft}_oauth.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">One per provider you generate</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">app/controllers/oauth_controller.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>/auth/:provider</code> + callback, <code>POST /auth/:provider/link</code>, <code>/oauth/identities</code>, <code>/oauth/reauth</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">app/views/oauth/identities.html.slv</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">The connected-accounts page</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">app/views/oauth/reauth.html.slv</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">&ldquo;Confirm it&rsquo;s you&rdquo; before a provider is linked</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">app/mailers/oauth_mailer.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">Tells the owner a provider was linked to their account (view in <code>app/views/oauth_mailer/</code>)</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">db/migrations/*_create_oauth_identities.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">Unique index on (provider, uid)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">The shared service is <code class="text-cyan-400">OauthSso</code> in <code>oauth_sso.sl</code>. It used to be <code>class OauthClient</code> in <code>oauth_client.sl</code>, which clashed with the <code>OauthClient</code> model <a href="/docs/security/oidc-provider" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">soli generate oidc_provider</a> writes &mdash; an app could not be both a client and a provider. The generator notes an old <code>oauth_client.sl</code> when it finds one: delete it, the <code>*_oauth.sl</code> services and <code>oauth_controller.sl</code>, then re-run it for each provider.</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Environment</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-2"><strong class="text-white">GitHub</strong></p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-bash text-sm">GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=…
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=…
GITHUB_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/auth/github/callback</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">The address is the one <code class="text-cyan-400">/user/emails</code> marks <strong class="text-white">primary and verified</strong> &mdash; never the profile&rsquo;s public <code>email</code>, which is whatever the user typed and GitHub never checked. The flow uses PKCE (S256). <code class="text-cyan-400">GITHUB_BASE_URL</code> / <code class="text-cyan-400">GITHUB_API_URL</code> point it at GitHub Enterprise Server.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-2"><strong class="text-white">Google</strong></p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-12">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-bash text-sm">GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=…
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=…
GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/auth/google/callback</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-2"><strong class="text-white">GitLab, Discord, LinkedIn, Microsoft</strong></p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">Each takes <code class="text-cyan-400">&lt;NAME&gt;_CLIENT_ID</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">&lt;NAME&gt;_CLIENT_SECRET</code> and <code class="text-cyan-400">&lt;NAME&gt;_REDIRECT_URI</code> (<code class="text-cyan-400">http://localhost:3000/auth/&lt;name&gt;/callback</code>). GitLab also reads <code class="text-cyan-400">GITLAB_BASE_URL</code> for a self-hosted instance &mdash; whose word on <code>email_verified</code> is then taken: its administrators can mark any address verified, and rule 3 below hands them the account with that address here. Point it only at gitlab.com or an instance you trust as you trust your own directory, as with Microsoft&rsquo;s named tenants. Discord and LinkedIn do not use PKCE: Discord does not document it, and LinkedIn offers it only to native apps it approves. <strong class="text-white">Microsoft requires <code class="text-cyan-400">MICROSOFT_TENANT</code></strong> (a tenant id), or the list of tenants below.</p>

    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-2"><strong class="text-white">Microsoft, several tenants</strong></p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-4">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-bash text-sm">MICROSOFT_TENANT=organizations
MICROSOFT_ALLOWED_TENANTS=&lt;tenant id&gt;,&lt;tenant id&gt;</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-8">Microsoft does not verify the <code class="text-cyan-400">email</code> claim, and a multi-tenant app can be handed someone else&rsquo;s address by a foreign tenant (the &ldquo;nOAuth&rdquo; takeover). So an address is only ever taken from a tenant you named: your own (<code class="text-cyan-400">MICROSOFT_TENANT=&lt;tenant id&gt;</code>), or the ones in <code class="text-cyan-400">MICROSOFT_ALLOWED_TENANTS</code>, each of which you trust as you trust your own. The tenant is read from the id_token (<code>tid</code>), whose issuer must be that tenant&rsquo;s. <code>common</code> and <code>consumers</code> are refused, and so is <code>organizations</code> without a list.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-8">Even inside a named tenant an administrator can give a user any <code>email</code>, so the address is used only when Microsoft says it is verified: <code class="text-cyan-400">email</code> with the <code class="text-cyan-400">xms_edov</code> claim <code>true</code> (the domain&rsquo;s owner is verified), or <code class="text-cyan-400">verified_primary_email</code>. Both are <strong class="text-white">optional claims you must add</strong>: Entra admin center &rarr; App registrations &rarr; your app &rarr; Token configuration &rarr; Add optional claim &rarr; token type <strong class="text-white">ID</strong> &rarr; <code>email</code>, <code>xms_edov</code>, <code>verified_primary_email</code>. Without them no address is trusted, and a Microsoft identity signs in only once a signed-in user has linked it at <code>/oauth/identities</code>. <code>preferred_username</code> is never taken as an address.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-8">The identity&rsquo;s uid is <code>&lt;tid&gt;:&lt;sub&gt;</code>: <code>sub</code> is unique only within one tenant. Apps generated before that stored the bare <code>sub</code>. <strong class="text-white">Upgrading:</strong> delete <code>app/services/microsoft_oauth.sl</code> and run <code class="text-cyan-400">soli generate oauth microsoft</code> (the generator notes an old file). The new service rewrites an old row to <code>&lt;tid&gt;:&lt;sub&gt;</code> the first time its owner signs in &mdash; when the <code>tid</code> recorded with the row (its <code>raw</code> claims) is the token&rsquo;s, or, for a row that recorded none, only in a single-tenant app: in a multi-tenant one the same <code>sub</code> may belong to a user of another directory. A row it does not adopt stays as it is, and its owner links Microsoft again from <code>/oauth/identities</code>.</p>

    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-2"><strong class="text-white">Apple</strong></p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-4">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-bash text-sm">APPLE_CLIENT_ID=com.example.app.web     # the Services ID
APPLE_TEAM_ID=…
APPLE_KEY_ID=…
APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE=config/AuthKey.p8   # inside the app; or APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY with its contents
APPLE_REDIRECT_URI=https://your.app/auth/apple/callback</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">Apple has no client secret to copy: it is a short-lived JWT the service signs with your key (ES256) for each exchange. Apple posts its answer (<code>response_mode=form_post</code>) from its own site, so the session cookie does not come with it; the callback turns that POST into a GET of the same URL &mdash; which the cookie does come with &mdash; and only then checks the state. <code>config/routes.sl</code> exempts <code class="text-cyan-400">/auth/apple/callback</code> from the CSRF gate for that one hop. The name arrives once, with the first sign-in; the email is in the id_token every time, and may be a private relay address. Apple accepts <code>https://</code> return URLs only, on a domain you verified with it.</p>

    <h2 id="accounts" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Which account a sign-in opens</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal list-inside text-gray-400 space-y-2 mb-4">
        <li>The account already linked to this <code>(provider, uid)</code>.</li>
        <li>While a signed-in user is connecting a provider, that user &mdash; refused when the identity belongs to someone else, when the account&rsquo;s email is not confirmed, without a recent sign-in, or when the account signs in through single sign-on only.</li>
        <li>The account with the address the provider <strong class="text-white">verified</strong>, linked from now on &mdash; but never one that signs in through single sign-on only, and never one already linked to another identity at this provider (the address was given to someone else there). An identity with no subject is refused outright, the sign-in fails when the identity cannot be stored, and an address that is not plain ASCII is never matched to an account (lowercasing folds look-alike letters into ASCII ones).</li>
        <li>A new one, when sign-up is open: <code class="text-cyan-400">AUTH_SIGNUP</code> applies as it does to <code>/signup</code> (<code>first_user</code> by default &mdash; the first account becomes the admin, then sign-up closes; <code>open</code>; <code>closed</code>). The account is confirmed and has no password; its owner can set one with a reset link.</li>
    </ol>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">Apple and Microsoft sign-ins carry a <code>nonce</code>: a random value sent with the authorization request and required in the id_token, so a token issued for another sign-in is refused. <code>MICROSOFT_TENANT</code> is a tenant id (a GUID) or <code>organizations</code> &mdash; no longer a domain, which could not be compared with the token &mdash; and the token&rsquo;s <code>tid</code> must be that id. <code>GITHUB_BASE_URL</code>, <code>GITHUB_API_URL</code> and <code>GITLAB_BASE_URL</code> must be <code>https://</code> (plain http only to this machine): over plain http the client secret and the code cross the network in clear. Disconnecting a provider needs a sign-in from the last ten minutes, like connecting one; the password check at <code>/oauth/reauth</code> shares the sign-in form&rsquo;s per-address limit; and what a provider or a query string says is logged on one line, without control characters.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">A provider that reports no verified address (an unverified Google, GitLab, LinkedIn, Apple or Discord address, a GitHub account without a verified primary one, a Microsoft token without <code>xms_edov</code> / <code>verified_primary_email</code>) still signs in through rule 1 &mdash; once it is linked &mdash; but never finds or creates an account by address.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">Rule 3 hands an existing account to whoever proves the address at the provider. If that account never confirmed its email, someone else may have created it in wait, with a password they know &mdash; and, before, providers they linked to it. So <code class="text-cyan-400">OauthSso.take_over_unconfirmed</code> calls <code class="text-cyan-400">User#revoke_credentials</code> (written by <code>soli generate auth</code>) before it is linked: the password and remember-me token are cleared, the session version bumped (every session ends), and the user&rsquo;s linked identities, passkeys, API tokens, two-factor setup and magic links are deleted. The address&rsquo;s owner gets the account alone. An app whose <code>user.sl</code> predates <code>revoke_credentials</code> gets a note from the generator.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4"><strong class="text-white">Single sign-on only.</strong> An account created by <a href="/docs/security/saml" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">soli generate saml</a> is marked <code>sso_only</code> (<code class="text-cyan-400">User#sso_only?</code>): it signs in through its identity provider and nothing else, so an administrator who removes someone there removes every way in. A provider vouching for the same address would be a way around that, so <code>OauthSso</code> refuses such an account in every rule &mdash; by address (rule 3), through an identity linked before it became SSO-only (rule 1), and when linking (rule 2). The check goes through <code>respond_to?("sso_only?")</code>, so an app whose <code>User</code> has no such method is unaffected.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">Whenever a provider is linked to an account that already existed &mdash; by its owner at <code>/oauth/identities</code> or by rule 3 &mdash; <code class="text-cyan-400">OauthMailer.provider_linked</code> emails the account&rsquo;s address, so an owner hears about a way in they did not add.</p>

    <h2 id="identities" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Connected accounts</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">/oauth/identities</code> lists the providers linked to the signed-in account, with a button to connect each provider the app has a service for and one to disconnect. The last way into an account cannot be removed: with no password, one provider stays.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4"><strong class="text-white">Connecting starts from a POST.</strong> Each Connect button is a <code class="text-cyan-400">button_to</code> form posting to <code class="text-cyan-400">POST /auth/:provider/link</code>, CSRF-checked like any other form. The plain <code>GET /auth/:provider</code> is for signing a <em>guest</em> in; a signed-in user who reaches it is shown <code>/oauth/identities</code> and nothing starts. It used to link: any site could send a recently signed-in visitor to <code>/auth/github</code> (a link, a top-level redirect), and the attacker&rsquo;s own GitHub account, authorized on the way, ended up attached to the victim&rsquo;s account. Starting a flow also drops any link left over from one abandoned half-way.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-2">Connecting needs:</p>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-gray-400 mb-4">
        <li><strong class="text-white">A confirmed email.</strong> An unconfirmed account (anyone can create one with any address) cannot link a provider: the POST answers 403.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">A recent sign-in</strong> &mdash; within ten minutes (<code class="text-cyan-400">OauthSso.reauth_seconds</code>). A provider sign-in counts; a password sign-in through <code>/login</code> is not timed by the auth scaffold, so the POST sends the user to <code class="text-cyan-400">/oauth/reauth</code>, which asks for the password (failures count toward the account lockout) and then goes straight on to the provider. An account with no password signs out and back in with a provider it has, then connects within ten minutes. Without this, a session borrowed for a minute &mdash; a shared computer, a stolen cookie &mdash; could attach a provider that keeps signing in after a password reset.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">An account that is not SSO-only</strong> (above): 403.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">An app generated before linking moved to the POST keeps its controller; the generator notes it. Delete <code>app/controllers/oauth_controller.sl</code>, <code>app/services/oauth_sso.sl</code> and <code>app/views/oauth/identities.html.slv</code>, then re-run it: the route <code class="text-cyan-400">post("/auth/:provider/link", "oauth#link_provider")</code> is appended under its own marker.</p>

    <h2 id="auth-time" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Sign-in time (<code>auth_time</code>)</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">A provider sign-in records <code class="text-cyan-400">Session.set("auth_time", DateTime.utc.to_unix)</code> (<code>OauthSso.sign_in</code>; linking does not, the session was already signed in). An app that is also an <a href="/docs/security/oidc-provider" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">OpenID Connect provider</a> puts it in its id_tokens and checks <code>max_age</code> against it.</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">How a provider is found</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12"><code class="text-cyan-400">/auth/:provider</code> looks up the <code class="text-cyan-400">&lt;Name&gt;Oauth</code> class by name after checking the segment against <code>^[a-z][a-z0-9]{1,19}$</code>, so adding a provider never means editing the controller. Each service exposes <code class="text-cyan-400">authorize_url(state, code_challenge)</code> and <code class="text-cyan-400">complete!(code)</code>, and the callback insists the provider matches the one this session started with. An app generated earlier has a controller with a fixed list; the generator says so, and deleting <code>oauth_controller.sl</code> then re-running it swaps in the new one.</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Login button</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">Add a link yourself (generator does not rewrite session views). A plain link is right here: it only ever signs a guest in.</p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-12">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-html text-sm">&lt;a href="/auth/github"&gt;Sign in with GitHub&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;a href="/auth/google"&gt;Sign in with Google&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Security</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-gray-400 mb-12">
        <li>Callback verifies <code class="text-cyan-400">state</code> against the session (CSRF)</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">PKCE is <code class="text-cyan-400">S256</code></strong> (every provider but Discord and LinkedIn).
            <code class="text-cyan-400">begin_pkce()</code> stores a random verifier in the session and returns
            <code class="text-cyan-400">base64url(SHA256(verifier))</code> unpadded, per RFC&nbsp;7636 &sect;4.2. Earlier cuts
            sent the raw verifier with <code class="text-cyan-400">code_challenge_method=plain</code>, where the challenge and
            the verifier are the same string &mdash; PKCE in name only. Never ship <code>plain</code>.</li>
        <li>Every value of a token request body is <code class="text-cyan-400">url_encode</code>d. Google&rsquo;s used to be concatenated raw, so a secret holding <code>&amp;</code>, <code>+</code> or <code>=</code> broke the exchange.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Errors are logged, not shown.</strong> A refusal written for the user (<code class="text-cyan-400">OauthSso.refuse</code>, e.g. &ldquo;this Apple account is linked to another user&rdquo;) reaches the page; what a provider sent &mdash; <code>error_description</code>, an error body, an address, a tenant id &mdash; goes to the log (<code>Logger.warn</code>) and the page says only that sign-in failed.</li>
        <li>The services call <code class="text-cyan-400">HTTP.request(method, url, headers, body)</code>.
            <code class="text-cyan-400">HTTP.get</code> / <code class="text-cyan-400">HTTP.post</code> read their options hash
            for <code>timeout</code> alone and return the body as a String, so a <code>"headers"</code> key there is silently
            dropped &mdash; which is why the generated flow used to 401. Provider responses are status-checked, so a 401
            reports as a 401 rather than a JSON parse error on the provider's error page.</li>
        <li>An address counts only when the provider verified it: Google, GitLab, LinkedIn and Apple report <code class="text-cyan-400">email_verified</code>; Discord <code class="text-cyan-400">verified</code>; GitHub marks it primary and verified in <code>/user/emails</code>; Microsoft needs <code class="text-cyan-400">xms_edov</code> or <code class="text-cyan-400">verified_primary_email</code>, from a tenant you name</li>
        <li>The start redirect uses <code class="text-cyan-400">redirect_external</code>: plain <code>redirect</code> only accepts local paths, and the first generated controller failed with a 500 for that reason</li>
        <li>OAuth-created accounts are confirmed and have no password, and the session carries the account&rsquo;s session version, like a password sign-in. Earlier cuts stored a random password in a plaintext <code>password</code> field, created accounts whatever <code class="text-cyan-400">AUTH_SIGNUP</code> said, and opened sessions that the first password reset on the account then refused</li>
        <li>Apple&rsquo;s and Microsoft&rsquo;s id_token is read without checking its signature: it comes from the provider&rsquo;s token endpoint over TLS, in answer to the server&rsquo;s own request (OpenID Connect Core &sect;3.1.3.7). Issuer, audience, expiry &mdash; and for Microsoft the tenant &mdash; are checked. That trust needs the answer to really be the provider&rsquo;s, so the token exchange follows no redirect, and <code>APPLE_BASE_URL</code> / <code>MICROSOFT_BASE_URL</code> may name only this machine (a test stand-in on <code>127.0.0.1</code>, <code>localhost</code> or <code>[::1]</code>); any other value is refused</li>
        <li>With <a href="/docs/security/authentication#two-factor" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">two-factor</a> on, a provider sign-in is followed by the code challenge</li>
        <li>Use HTTPS redirect URIs in production</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Ceiling</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        Seven providers: <strong class="text-white">Apple, GitHub, Google, GitLab, Discord, LinkedIn</strong> and <strong class="text-white">Microsoft</strong>. No OmniAuth-style catalog. Microsoft personal accounts (<code>consumers</code>) are not supported, and neither is an open multi-tenant app (any directory). Apple&rsquo;s &ldquo;hide my email&rdquo; relay address is taken as the account&rsquo;s email as it is. An app generated before account linking keeps its controller and service: delete <code>oauth_controller.sl</code> and <code>oauth_client.sl</code>, then re-run the generator.
        Longer walkthroughs:
        <a href="/docs/blog/github-oauth" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">GitHub</a>,
        <a href="/docs/blog/google-oauth" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Google</a>.
    </p>
</div>
